\ read(2) and write(2), once each: n is the count transferred, or a
\ NEGATIVE errno. Everything that reads or writes a descriptor is
\ Forth on top of these two - KEY, ACCEPT, READ-FILE, READ-LINE,
\ WRITE-FILE, WRITE-LINE - so the policy about buffering and about
\ which errors to retry is in one place, visible (Iteration 245). Both
\ flush the engine's terminal output buffer first, which keeps a
\ prompt ahead of the input it asks for and TYPE's output in order
\ with every other write.
PRIMITIVE READ      ( c-addr u fd --- n )
PRIMITIVE WRITE     ( c-addr u fd --- n )
\ poll(2), once: n structs pollfd at a-addr, waiting ms milliseconds
\ (-1: for ever). n' is the number ready, 0 on timeout, or a negative
\ errno. Flushes terminal output first, like READ. See FD-POLL.
PRIMITIVE POLL      ( a-addr n ms --- n' )
\ The terminal on fd a byte at a time without echo (flag true), or back
\ as it was (flag false): 0 or a negative errno. Ctrl-C still works, and
\ the engine puts the terminal back on every way out. See KEY?.
PRIMITIVE RAW-MODE  ( fd flag --- ior )
