\ PART 3: SIMPLE DEFINITIONS

\ This is a large class of words, which would be written in machine code
\ on most non-native code systems. Many contain just a few words, so they
\ are implemented as macros.

\ This category contains simple arithmetic and compare words, the runtime
\ parts of DO LOOP  and string related words etc, many on which are
\ dependent on each other, so they are in a less than logical order to
\ avoid large numbers of forward references.






: EMIT ( c --- )
\ Output the character c to the terminal.
\
\ No buffer: the character is already in memory, on the data stack,
\ and SP@ is its address. TYPE reads one byte from there and DROP
\ removes it. A VARIABLE for this cost a dictionary header and a cell
\ in every image to hold a byte that was already sitting in one.
\
\ Little-endian only, since the low byte of the cell has to be the one
\ at SP@ - which this system is anyway, by GOALS.md's non-goals and by
\ the image format. Checked on both engines, including the one that
\ caches the top of stack in a register: TYPE spills it below SP@'s
\ result, so the address stays good.
   SP@ 1 TYPE DROP ;


: CR  ( --- )
\ Output a newline to the terminal.
   13 EMIT 10 EMIT ;

VARIABLE START \ holds absolute address of the system address space

VARIABLE BOOT
\ Turnkey hook, 0 in a plain kernel image. When nonzero it holds the
\ execution token of a word to run at startup, stored as an OFFSET
\ from START rather than an absolute address - so it survives being
\ written to disk and reloaded at a different address, and COLD adds
\ START back when it runs it rather than relocating it in place (which
\ would make the value absolute and so wrong if the image were saved
\ again). Set via save-system.4's SET-BOOT; see PROGRESS.md's
\ Iteration 40 entry for why this exists - a prebuilt shell image
\ boots straight into shell.4's MAIN, skipping both the banner and
\ ~253ms of recompiling shell.4 on every single invocation.

-1 CONSTANT TRUE

VARIABLE S0 ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the bottom address of the stack.

VARIABLE R0 ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the bottom address of the return stack.

: DEPTH ( --- n )
\ n is the number of cells on the stack (before DEPTH was executed).
  SP@ S0 @ SWAP - CELLSHIFT-TOK RSHIFT ;



\ The next few words manipulate addresses in a system-independent way.
\ Use CHAR+ instead of 1+ and it will be portable to systems where you
\ have to add something different from 1.


: CHARS ( n1 --- n2)
\ n2 is the number of address units occupied by n1 characters.
; \ A no-op.



: CELL- ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2)
\ a-addr2 is the address of the previous cell before a-addr1.
   CELLBYTES-TOK - ;

\ The DO LOOP related words use the return stack. The top of the
\ return stack is the loop counter (I) and the next cell is the limit.

\ (LOOP) and (+LOOP) are followed by a branch back to the loop's start,
\ and (?DO) by a branch to its exit (Iteration 258; each was followed by
\ an inline cell until then). They decide by where they RETURN: into the
\ branch, to take it, or past it. The branch is two bytes or three.
\ These words are called 'subroutines', not macros.

\ To access the loop parameters on the return stack, the DO LOOP words must
\ first pop their OWN return address!

: SKIP-BRANCH ( a --- a' )
\ Past the branch instruction at a.
  DUP C@ BRANCH8-TOK = IF 2 ELSE 3 THEN + ;

: (DO)   ( n1 n2 ---)
\ Runtime part of DO.
  R> ROT ROT SWAP >R >R >R ;
: (?DO)  ( n1 n2 ---)
\ Runtime part of ?DO. An empty loop returns into the branch to the exit;
\ any other is entered past it.
   2DUP = IF 2DROP EXIT THEN
   R> ROT ROT SWAP >R >R SKIP-BRANCH >R ;
: I ( --- n )
\ Return the counter (index) of the innermost DO LOOP
     R> R@ SWAP >R ;

: J  ( --- n)
\ Return the counter (index) of the next loop outer to the innermost DO LOOP
  RP@ CELLBYTES-TOK CELLBYTES-TOK CELLBYTES-TOK + + + @ ;

: UNLOOP ( --- )
\ Remove one set of loop parameters from the return stack. LEAVE is this
\ and a branch to the loop's exit.
   R> R> R> 2DROP >R ;

: (LOOP) ( ---)
\ Runtime part of LOOP
  R> R> 1 + DUP R@ =   \ Add 1 to count and compare to limit.
  IF
   R> 2DROP SKIP-BRANCH >R \ Discard parameters and step over the branch.
  ELSE
   >R >R           \ Repush counter, and return into the branch back.
  THEN ;

: (+LOOP) ( n ---)
\ Runtime part of +LOOP
\ Very similar to (LOOP), but the compare condition is different.
\ With x = index - limit, the loop has crossed the boundary between
\ limit-1 and limit exactly when x changes sign AND x and the step n
\ have opposite signs:  ((x XOR x+n) AND (x XOR n)) < 0.
\ That is the signed-overflow test on the biased index x+MIN-INT,
\ computed without storing the bias, so (DO), I, J and the rest are
\ unchanged. The previous test used only the first half - "x changed
\ sign" - which also fires when x wraps from MAX-INT to MIN-INT, so a
\ step of a 256th of the cell range stopped half way (Iteration 243;
\ coreplustest.fth, seven cases).
     R> SWAP R>              \ ret n index
     2DUP + ROT ROT          \ ret index' n index
     R@ -                    \ ret index' n x
     2DUP XOR >R             \ ret index' n x        R: x^n limit
     DUP ROT +               \ ret index' x x'
     XOR R> AND 0 <          \ ret index' crossed?
     IF R> 2DROP SKIP-BRANCH >R
     ELSE >R >R THEN ;



: (.") ( --- )
\ Runtime part of ."
\ This expects an in-line counted string.
  R> COUNT 2DUP TYPE + >R ;

: (S")  ( --- c-addr u )
\ Runtime part of S"
\ It returns address and length of an in-line counted string.
  R> COUNT 2DUP + >R ;

32 CONSTANT BL ( --- 32 )
\ Constant 32, the blank character

: PICK  ( u --- x)
\ place a copy of stack cell number u on the stack. 0 PICK is DUP, 1 PICK
\ is OVER etc.
  1 + CELLS SP@ + @ ;




: 2* ( w1 --- w2)
\ Multiply w1 by 2.
  1 LSHIFT ;

: 2/  ( n1 --- n2)
\ Divide signed number n1 by 2.
  \ Written as a computed mask (1 SIGNSHIFT-TOK LSHIFT), not a literal:
  \ the cross-compiler's host may itself have narrower cells than the
  \ target - see PROGRESS.md - and the shift amount itself depends on
  \ TARGET-CELL-BYTES (63 for 8-byte cells, 31 for 4-byte).
  DUP 1 SIGNSHIFT-TOK LSHIFT AND SWAP 1 RSHIFT OR ;

: +! ( w a-addr ---)
\ Add w to the contents of the cell at a-addr.
   DUP @ ROT + SWAP ! ;

\ Double numbers occupy two cells in memory and on the stack.
\ The most significant half on the number is in the first memory
\ cell or in the top cell on the stack (which is also the first address).

: 2@ ( a-addr --- d )
\ Fetch double number d at a-addr.
   DUP CELL+ @ SWAP @ ;

: 2! ( d a-addr --- )
\ Store the double number d at a-addr.
   DUP >R ! R> CELL+ ! ;

: ?DUP ( n --- 0 | n n)
\ Duplicate the top cell on the stack, but only if it is nonzero.
   DUP IF DUP THEN ;

: MIN ( n1 n2 --- n3)
\ n3 is the minimum of n1 and n2.
   2DUP > IF SWAP THEN DROP ;

: MAX ( n1 n2 --- n3)
\ n3 is the maximum of n1 and n2.
   2DUP < IF SWAP THEN DROP ;

: DNEGATE ( d1 --- d2)
\ Negate the top double number on the stack.
   >R NEGATE R> NEGATE OVER 0= 0=  + ;

: ABS ( n --- u)
\ u is the absolute value of n.
   DUP 0< IF NEGATE THEN ;

: DABS ( d --- ud)
\ ud is the absolute value of d.
   DUP 0< IF DNEGATE THEN ;

: SM/REM ( d n1 --- nrem nquot )
\ Divide signed double number d by single number n1, giving quotient and
\ remainder. Round towards zero, remainder has same sign as dividend.
  2DUP  XOR >R OVER >R \ Push signs of quot and rem.
  ABS >R DABS R>
  UM/MOD
  SWAP R> 0< IF NEGATE THEN SWAP
  R> 0< IF NEGATE THEN ;

: FM/MOD ( d n1 --- nrem nquot )
\ Divide signed double number d by single number n1, giving quotient and
\ remainder. Round always down (floored division),
\ remainder has same sign as divisor.
  DUP >R 2DUP XOR >R
  SM/REM
  OVER R> 0< AND IF SWAP R@ + SWAP 1 - THEN R> DROP ;

: M* ( n1 n2 --- d )
\ Multiply the signed numbers n1 and n2, giving the signed double number d.
  2DUP  XOR >R ABS SWAP ABS UM* R> 0< IF DNEGATE THEN ;

: * ( w1 w2 --- w3)
\ Multiply single numbers, signed or unsigned give the same result.
  UM* DROP ;

: */MOD ( n1 n2 n3 --- nrem nquot)
\ Multiply signed numbers n1 by n2 and divide by n3, giving quotient and
\ remainder. Intermediate result is double.
  >R M* R> FM/MOD ;

: */    ( n1 n2 n3 --- n4 )
\ Multiply signed numbers n1 by n2 and divide by n3, giving quotient n4.
\ Intermediate result is double.
  */MOD SWAP DROP ;

: S>D  ( n --- d)
\ Convert single number to double number.
   DUP 0< ;

: /MOD  ( n1 n2 --- nrem nquot)
\ Divide signed number n1 by n2, giving quotient and remainder.
   SWAP S>D ROT FM/MOD ;

: / ( n1 n2 --- n3)
\ n3 is n1 divided by n2.
 /MOD SWAP DROP ;

: MOD ( n1 n2 --- n3)
\ n3 is the remainder of n1 and n2.
  /MOD DROP ;

: EXECUTE ( xt ---)
\ Execute the word with execution token xt.
\ Return from EXECUTE goes to xt pushed on the ret stack by >R, return from
\ the word x1 returns to definition that calls EXECUTE
  >R ;

\ PART 4: NUMERIC OUTPUT WORDS.

VARIABLE BASE ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that contains the numerical conversion base.

VARIABLE DP   ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that contains the dictionary pointer. New space is allocated
\ from the address in DP

VARIABLE HLD ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the address of the numerical output conversion
\ character.

VARIABLE DPL ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the decimal point location for numerical conversion.

: DECIMAL ( --- )
\ Set numerical conversion to decimal.
  10 BASE ! ;

: HEX     ( --- )
\ Set numerical conversion to hexadecimal.
  16 BASE ! ;

: SPACE  ( ---)
\ Output a space to the terminal.
  BL EMIT ;

: SPACES ( u --- )
\ Output u spaces to the terminal.
  BEGIN DUP WHILE SPACE 1- REPEAT DROP ;

: HERE ( --- c-addr )
\ The address of the dictionary pointer. New space is allocated here.
  DP @ ;

: PAD ( --- c-addr )
\ The address of a scratch pad area. Right below this address there is
\ the numerical conversion buffer.
  \ 84 was enough headroom for a 32-bit-cell double number in base 2 (65
  \ digits). With 8-byte cells a double number can need up to 129 digits
  \ in base 2, so this is widened accordingly; see GOALS.md.
  DP @ 160 + ;

: MU/MOD ( ud u --- urem udquot )
\ Divide unsigned double number ud by u and return a double quotient and
\ a single remainder.
  >R 0 R@ UM/MOD R> SWAP >R UM/MOD R> 
;

\ The numerical conversion buffer starts right below PAD and grows down.
\ Characters are added to it from right to left, as as the div/mod algorithm
\ to convert numbers to an arbitrary base produces the digits from right to
\ left.

: HOLD ( c ---)
\ Insert character c into the numerical conversion buffer.
  -1 HLD +! HLD @ C! ;

: # ( ud1 --- ud2)
\ Extract the rightmost digit of ud1 and put it into the numerical
\ conversion buffer.
  BASE @ MU/MOD ROT DUP 9 > IF 7 + THEN 48 + HOLD ;

: #S ( ud --- 0 0 )
\ Convert ud by repeated use of # until ud is zero.
  BEGIN # 2DUP OR 0= UNTIL ;

: SIGN ( n ---)
\ Insert a - sign in the numerical conversion buffer if n is negative.
  0< IF 45 HOLD THEN ;

: <# ( --- )
\ Reset the numerical conversion buffer.
  PAD HLD ! ;

: #> ( ud --- addr u )
\ Discard ud and give the address and length of the numerical conversion
\ buffer.
  2DROP HLD @ PAD OVER - ;

: D. ( d --- )
\ Type the double number d to the terminal.
  SWAP OVER DABS <# #S ROT SIGN #> TYPE SPACE ;

: U. ( u ---)
\ Type the unsigned number u to the terminal.
  0 D. ;

: . ( n ---)
\ Type the signed number n to the terminal.
  S>D D. ;

\ PART 5: MEMORY BLOCK MOVE AND RELATED WORDS.

: CMOVE ( c-addr1 c-addr2 u --- )
\ Copy u bytes starting at c-addr1 to c-addr2, proceeding in ascending
\ order.
   DUP IF >R
   BEGIN
    OVER C@ SWAP DUP >R C! R> 1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP
    R> -1 + DUP >R 0=
   UNTIL
   R>
   THEN
   2DROP DROP
;

: CMOVE> ( c-addr1 c-addr2 u --- )
\ Copy a block of u bytes starting at c-addr1 to c-addr2, proceeding in
\ descending order.
   DUP IF >R R@ + -1 + SWAP R@ + -1 + SWAP
   BEGIN
    OVER C@ SWAP DUP >R C! R> -1 + SWAP -1 + SWAP
    R> -1 + DUP >R 0=
   UNTIL
   R>
   THEN
   2DROP DROP
;

\ It's here because it needs CMOVE>
: ROLL ( u ---)
\  Move stack cell number u to the top. 1 ROLL is SWAP, 2 ROLL is ROT etc.
  1 + CELLS DUP SP@ + CELL+ @ SWAP
  SP@ CELL+ DUP CELL+ ROT MOVE DROP ;



: 2OVER ( d1 d2 --- d1 d2 d1)
\ Take a copy of the second double number of the stack and push it on the
\ stack.
  03 PICK 03 PICK ;

: 2SWAP ( d1 d2 --- d2 d1)
\ Swap the top two double numbers on the stack. It was `3 ROLL 3 ROLL`,
\ and ROLL moves the stack a byte at a time: 41% of the dispatches in
\ the shell's loop benchmark were those bytes (Iteration 260).
  ROT >R ROT R> ;

\ PART 6: FILE ACCESS WORDS.

00 CONSTANT W/O ( --- mode)
\ Read only file access mode.

02 CONSTANT R/O ( --- mode)
\ Write only file access mode.

04 CONSTANT R/W ( --- mode)
\ Read write file access mode.

06 CONSTANT A/O ( --- mode)
10 CONSTANT X/O ( --- mode)   \ create, but not over an existing file
\ Write only, append (create if missing, never truncate) file access
\ mode - added for shell.4's ">>" redirection (see PROGRESS.md,
\ Iteration 7); none of the three modes above create a missing file
\ without also truncating it if it already exists.

: BIN ( mode1 --- mode2)
\ Modify the R/O W/O or R/W mode so that it applies to binary files.
  1 + ;


: CREATE-FILE ( c-addr u mode --- fid ior)
\ Create a new file with the name starting at c-addr with length u,
\ truncating any that exists, and open it in the given mode. Return
\ the file-ID and the IO result (ior=0 if success). W/O and A/O keep
\ their own OPEN-FILE modes; R/O and R/W use the engine's read-write
\ create modes, 8 and 9 (cv8.c's open_flags). Until Iteration 245 this
\ masked every mode to W/O, and a file created R/W was write-only.
  DUP 2 U< 0= OVER 6 U< AND IF 1 AND 8 + THEN OPEN-FILE ;

\ : READ-LINE >R OVER R> READ-FILE >R OVER + SWAP 
\ BEGIN WHILE REPEAT 
\ R> ;

\ PART 7: SOURCE INPUT WORDS.

VARIABLE TIB ( --- addr)
\ is the standard terminal input buffer.
80 CHARS-T ALLOT-T

VARIABLE #TIB ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the number of characters in the terminal input buffer.

VARIABLE >IN  ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds an index in the current input source where the next word
\ will be parsed.

VARIABLE SID  ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the source i.d. returned by SOURCE-ID.

VARIABLE SRC  ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the address of the current input source.

VARIABLE #SRC ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the length of the current input source.


: READ-SOME ( c-addr u fd --- n )
\ One read(2), retried when a signal interrupted it (EINTR, 4): n bytes,
\ 0 at end of file, or a negative errno.
  BEGIN >R 2DUP R@ READ DUP -4 = WHILE DROP R> REPEAT
  R> DROP >R 2DROP R> ;

: READ-FILE ( c-addr u1 fid --- u2 ior )
\ Read u1 characters, fewer only at end of file. read(2) returns less
\ than asked for on a pipe or a terminal, so this loops. ior is 0 or a
\ negative errno.
  >R 0 >R                                ( c-addr u1  R: fid got )
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    2DUP R> R@ SWAP >R READ-SOME         ( c-addr u1 n )
    DUP 0< IF >R 2DROP R> R> SWAP R> DROP EXIT THEN
    DUP 0= IF DROP 2DROP R> 0 R> DROP EXIT THEN
    R> OVER + >R
    ROT OVER + ROT ROT -
  REPEAT
  2DROP R> 0 R> DROP ;

: WRITE-FILE ( c-addr u fid --- ior )
\ Write all u characters, looping over short writes. ior is 0, a
\ negative errno, or -1 if write(2) made no progress.
  >R
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    2DUP R@ WRITE                        ( c-addr u n )
    DUP -4 = IF DROP ELSE
      DUP 0 > 0= IF >R 2DROP R> DUP 0= + R> DROP EXIT THEN
      ROT OVER + ROT ROT -
    THEN
  REPEAT
  2DROP R> DROP 0 ;

: WRITE-LINE ( c-addr u fid --- ior )
\ Write the string and a newline. The newline is the 10 on the stack,
\ written from where SP@ says it is, as EMIT does.
  DUP >R WRITE-FILE ?DUP IF R> DROP EXIT THEN
  10 SP@ 1 R> WRITE-FILE SWAP DROP ;

VARIABLE RL-NEWLINE?   \ did the line just read end at a newline

: RL-END ( c-addr p nl? flag ior --- u2 flag ior )
\ READ-LINE's result from its end pointer p. When the line ended at a
\ newline (nl?), a CR just before it is part of the terminator: CRLF
\ files read as plain lines. A line cut short by the buffer keeps a
\ trailing CR, because nothing says it is the end: stripping it made a
\ full buffer look one short, and a caller growing its buffer took the
\ line for finished (Iteration 249).
  >R >R >R OVER -
  R> DUP RL-NEWLINE? !                   \ the shell's `read` needs this
  IF DUP IF 2DUP + 1- C@ 13 = IF 1- THEN THEN THEN
  SWAP DROP R> R> ;

: RL-BYTES ( c-addr u1 fid --- u2 flag ior )
\ READ-LINE a byte at a time, for a descriptor that cannot seek: a
\ byte read past the newline could not be given back, and a pipe or a
\ terminal is shared with whatever runs next.
  >R OVER + OVER                         ( c-addr end p  R: fid )
  BEGIN 2DUP - WHILE
    DUP 1 R@ READ-SOME                   ( c-addr end p n )
    DUP 1 = IF
      DROP DUP C@ 10 = IF SWAP DROP -1 -1 0 R> DROP RL-END EXIT THEN
      1+
    ELSE
      \ 0 is end of file, below 0 an error; the flag says whether this
      \ line got any characters before it.
      >R SWAP DROP 2DUP - 0= 0=          ( c-addr p any  R: fid n )
      0 SWAP R> R> DROP                  ( c-addr p 0 any n )
      DUP 0< 0= IF DROP 0 THEN
      RL-END EXIT
    THEN
  REPEAT
  SWAP DROP 0 -1 0 R> DROP RL-END ;

: READ-LINE ( c-addr u1 fid --- u2 flag ior )
\ Read a line of at most u1 characters; the newline ends it and is not
\ stored. flag is false only at end of file.
\
\ No buffer. A descriptor that can seek is read a block at a time, and
\ whatever was read past the newline is given back by seeking to just
\ after it - so the file offset is always exactly where the reader has
\ consumed to, and nothing else needs to know a read happened. That is
\ what the engine's read-ahead pool achieved with a table, an LRU and
\ seek-back on eviction (until Iteration 245). Anything else is read a
\ byte at a time: see RL-BYTES.
  OVER 0= IF 2DROP DROP 0 -1 0 EXIT THEN   \ zero characters: not EOF
  DUP FILE-POSITION IF 2DROP RL-BYTES EXIT THEN
  >R >R >R                               ( c-addr u1  R: hi lo fid )
  OVER SWAP R@ READ-FILE                 ( c-addr n ior )
  ?DUP IF >R DROP DUP 0 -1 R> R> DROP R> R> 2DROP RL-END EXIT THEN
  DUP 0= IF DROP DUP 0 0 0 R> DROP R> R> 2DROP RL-END EXIT THEN
  2DUP 10 SCAN SWAP DROP                 ( c-addr n rest )
  DUP >R - R>                            ( c-addr i rest )
  DUP 0= 0= ROT ROT                      ( c-addr nl? i rest )
  1 > IF                                 \ read past the newline: give it back
    R> R> R>                             ( c-addr nl? i fid lo hi )
    3 PICK 1+ 0 D+ ROT REPOSITION-FILE   ( c-addr nl? i ior )
  ELSE
    R> R> R> 2DROP DROP 0                ( c-addr nl? i 0 )
  THEN
  >R ROT DUP ROT + ROT -1 R> RL-END ;

: W! ( w a-addr --- )
\ Store the low 16 bits of w at a-addr, little-endian.
  2DUP C! SWAP 8 RSHIFT SWAP 1+ C! ;

: W@ ( a-addr --- w )
\ Fetch an unsigned 16-bit little-endian value.
  DUP C@ SWAP 1+ C@ 8 LSHIFT OR ;

: FD-POLL ( fd events ms --- revents | 0 | -errno )
\ poll(2) on one descriptor: the events that occurred, 0 on timeout, or
\ a negative errno. events and revents are poll(2)'s bits; POLLIN is 1.
\
\ The struct pollfd - an int fd, then short events and short revents,
\ eight bytes, checked by cv8.c at build time - is laid out in two
\ cells pushed onto the data stack for the purpose and found with SP@,
\ as KEY finds its byte. No variable, so the word is reentrant, which a
\ multitasker will need.
  >R >R >R 0 0 SP@ R> R> R>            ( 0 0 p fd events ms )
  >R 2 PICK 4 + W!                     ( 0 0 p fd )
  DUP 2 PICK W! 16 RSHIFT OVER 2 + W!  ( 0 0 p )
  DUP 1 R> POLL                        ( 0 0 p n )
  DUP 0 > IF DROP 6 + W@ ELSE SWAP DROP THEN
  >R 2DROP R> ;

: FD-WAIT ( fd events --- revents )
\ Wait for one of events on fd, however long it takes: the events that
\ occurred, or 0 if poll(2) itself failed. A signal is retried.
  BEGIN 2DUP -1 FD-POLL DUP -4 = WHILE DROP REPEAT
  >R 2DROP R> 0 MAX ;

: KEY ( --- c )
\ Receive one character from the terminal.
\
\ One byte, unbuffered, straight off descriptor 0 - which is what
\ gforth, SPF and lbForth all do, and what dash does for its `read`
\ builtin. No buffer, and no buffer is possible: descriptor 0 is
\ INHERITED, shared with every child, and a byte read ahead is a byte
\ taken from whatever runs next. The engine used to hold 4096 bytes of
\ it, and `read x; cat` swallowed the whole pipe - see
\ tests/shell/run-read. Reading into the cell the 0 just pushed, via
\ SP@, for the same reason EMIT does.
\
\ End of input exits, which is not decoration: without it a shell
\ reading a script off stdin spun on read(0,"",1)=0 forever (GOALS.md's
\ Bug 3).
\
\ A failed read WAITS, once, for descriptor 0 to become readable, then
\ tries again; a second failure exits. That covers a non-blocking
\ descriptor with nothing typed yet (EAGAIN, where ANS KEY must wait
\ and this used to exit), without knowing EAGAIN's value, which is 11
\ on Linux and 35 on the BSDs. A real error - stdin a directory, a
\ terminal gone - leaves the descriptor "readable", so the retry fails
\ at once and KEY exits rather than spinning. No shared state changes:
\ fcntl could have made the descriptor blocking, but the flag belongs
\ to every process sharing it (Iteration 246).
  0                                    ( waited? )
  BEGIN
    0 SP@ 1 0 READ-SOME                ( waited? c n )
    DUP 1 = IF DROP SWAP DROP EXIT THEN
    0= ROT OR IF BYE THEN              ( c )
    DROP 0 1 FD-WAIT 1 AND 0= IF BYE THEN
    -1
  0 UNTIL ;

: KEY? ( --- flag )
\ Is a character available from the terminal, now? Also true at end of
\ input or on an error, where KEY would not wait either. On a terminal
\ in its usual line mode the system sees nothing until Enter; after
\ `0 -1 RAW-MODE` it sees each key as it is typed.
  0 1 0 FD-POLL 0= 0= ;

: MS ( u --- )
\ Wait at least u milliseconds: poll(2) on no descriptors. A signal can
\ end the wait early; nothing in this system installs a handler.
  0 0 ROT POLL DROP ;

: ACCEPT ( c-addr n1 --- n2 )
\ Read a line from the terminal into a buffer at c-addr of length n1;
\ n2 is the number of characters read. Backspace and DEL erase one
\ character if there is one, CR and LF end the line and are not
\ stored, and a character that would overflow the buffer is dropped.
\
\ ANS requires this to WAIT for a line terminator, which is why it
\ cannot be the non-blocking read; that is READ-STDIN, and the standard's
\ own answer for "read whatever is ready" is KEY? - which this system
\ does not have yet.
  >R 0
  BEGIN
   KEY DUP 8 = OVER 127 = OR
   IF
     DROP DUP IF 1- THEN
   ELSE
     DUP 10 = OVER 13 = OR
     IF
      DROP SWAP DROP R> DROP EXIT
     ELSE
      OVER R@ - IF
       >R OVER OVER + R> SWAP C! 1+
      ELSE
       DROP
      THEN
     THEN
   THEN
  0 UNTIL
;

: QUERY ( --- )
\ Read a line from the terminal into the terminal input buffer.
  TIB 80 ACCEPT #TIB ! ;

: SOURCE ( --- addr len)
\ Return the address and length of the current input source.
   SRC @ #SRC @ ;

: SOURCE-ID ( --- sid)
\ Return the i.d. of the current source i.d., 0 for terminal, -1
\ for EVALUATE and positive number for INCLUDE file.
   SID @ ;

: REFILL ( --- f)
\ Refill the current input source when it is exhausted. f is
\ true if it was successfully refilled.
  SOURCE-ID -1 = IF
   0 \ Not refillable for EVALUATE
  ELSE
   SOURCE-ID IF
    SRC @ 256 SOURCE-ID READ-LINE ABORT" Error reading file"
    SWAP #SRC ! 0 >IN !
    #SRC @ IF SOURCE OVER + SWAP 
    BEGIN 2DUP - WHILE DUP C@ 9 = IF BL OVER C! THEN 1+ REPEAT 2DROP THEN
    \ Change tabs to space.
    \ flag from READ-LINE is returned (no success at EOF)
   ELSE
    QUERY #TIB @ #SRC ! 0 >IN ! -1 \ Always successful from terminal.
   THEN
  THEN
;

: SKIP ( c-addr1 u1 c --- c-addr2 u2 )
\ Find the first character not equal to c in the string c-addr1 u1
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remaining part of the string starting with the
\ nonmatching char. It is a zero-length string if no other chars found.
  BEGIN
   OVER
  WHILE
   ROT DUP C@ >R OVER R> -
   IF
    ROT ROT DROP EXIT
   THEN
   1+ ROT ROT SWAP 1- SWAP
  REPEAT DROP
;

: PARSE ( c --- c-addr u )
\ Parse ccc delimited by c from the input source and step past the
\ delimiter. Leading delimiters are NOT skipped - that is WORD's job,
\ and the standard's. Until Iteration 245 this skipped them, as SOD32's
\ does, so S" " took the closing quote for a leading delimiter and
\ swallowed the rest of the line as the string.
  >R SOURCE SWAP >IN @ + SWAP >IN @ -   ( a n )
  OVER SWAP R> SCAN                     ( a a2 n2 )
  IF 1 >IN +! THEN
  OVER - DUP >IN +! ;

: PLACE ( addr len c-addr --- )
\ Place the string starting at addr with length len at c-addr as
\ a counted string.
  2DUP C! CHAR+ SWAP CMOVE ;

: WORD ( c --- addr )
\ Skip leading c, parse to the next c, and leave the result as a
\ counted string at HERE, followed by a blank.
  >R SOURCE SWAP >IN @ + SWAP >IN @ -   ( a n )
  R@ SKIP DROP SOURCE DROP - >IN !
  R> PARSE HERE PLACE HERE BL HERE COUNT + C! ;

\ PART 8: INTERPRETER HELPER WORDS

\ First we need FIND and related words.

\ Each word list is hashed into threads, each a linked list of
\ definitions, newest first; a search walks only the thread its name
\ hashes to (see HASH). Names are compared a byte at a time; they are
\ not padded to a cell (Iteration 243).

\ Dictionary definitions are built as follows:
\
\ LINK field: 1-3 bytes, the distance back to the name field of the
\             previous word in the same thread; see PREV-NFA.
\ NAME field: counted string of at most 31 characters, not padded.
\             bits 5-7 of length byte have special meaning.
\                   7 is always set to mark start of name ( for >NAME)
\                   6 is set if the word is immediate.
\                   5 is set if the word is a macro.
\ CODE field: the byte after the name, is execution token for word.
\             here the executable code for the word starts. Not aligned.
\ PARAMETER field: (body) Contains the data of variables etc.; always
\             cell-aligned, at align(xt+4) - see CREATE.

VARIABLE NAMEBUF ( --- a-addr)
\ An aligned buffer that holds a copy of the name that is searched.
28 ALLOT-T

VARIABLE WORDLISTS ( --- a-addr)
\ Head of the chain of wordlists OTHER than FORTH-WORDLIST: the
\ START-relative offset of the most recently created one, or 0.
\
\ Each such wordlist carries the offset of the next in a link cell
\ immediately BEFORE its count cell, so the chain costs one cell per
\ wordlist and needs no table and no limit. Offsets, not addresses, so
\ the chain itself never needs relocating - only the thread heads
\ inside each wordlist do.
\
\ COLD relocates every wordlist on this chain and SAVE-SYSTEM
\ unrelocates the same walk. Before it existed both knew about exactly
\ one wordlist, which was complete for as long as there WAS one: the
\ moment extend.4 was loaded into a saved image, ROOT-WORDLIST and
\ every VOCABULARY kept the builder's absolute addresses, and two
\ identical builds differed in eight cells by exactly the ASLR delta.

VARIABLE FORTH-WORDLIST ( --- addr)
\ A hashed word list, laid out as SOD32's is: cell 0 holds the number
\ of threads and cells 1..n the head of each thread.
\
\ RelF carried this structure over from SOD32 as a SINGLE cell - "a
\ pointer to the last definition" - which made every lookup a linear
\ scan of the whole dictionary. Measured on bench/parse.fth that cost
\ 8.05 million dictionary entries visited against SOD32's 397 thousand,
\ and about 81% of every operation the system executed. See
\ FINDINGS-OUTER-INTERPRETER.md.
32 CELLS-T ALLOT-T

VARIABLE LAST ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds a pointer to the last definition created.

VARIABLE CONTEXT 28 ALLOT-T ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable holds the addresses of up to 8 word lists that are
\ in the search order.

VARIABLE #ORDER ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the number of word list that are in the search order.

VARIABLE CURRENT ( --- addr)
\ This variable holds the address of the word list to which new definitions
\ are added.

: HASH ( c-addr u #threads --- n )
\ SOD32's hash, unchanged: the length and the first two characters.
\ It must agree EXACTLY with cross.4's THASH, which threads the
\ dictionary of the image being built, or a word would be filed under
\ one thread and looked for under another.
  >R OVER C@ 1 LSHIFT OVER 1 > IF ROT 1+ C@ 2 LSHIFT XOR ELSE ROT DROP
   THEN XOR
  R> 1- AND ;

: >NAME ( addr1 --- addr2 )
\ Convert execution token addr1 (address of code) to address of name:
\ the count byte is the only header byte with bit 7 set that is reached
\ scanning back from the body, since names are 7-bit.
  BEGIN 1- DUP C@ 128 AND UNTIL ;

: NAME> ( addr1 --- addr2 )
\ Convert address of name to address of code: the body follows the
\ name directly.
  COUNT 31 AND + ;

: NAME>BUF ( c-addr u ---)
\ Move the name c-addr u to the buffer NAMEBUF.
  NAMEBUF 32 0 FILL 32 MIN NAMEBUF PLACE ;

\ A dictionary header is [link][count][name][body], with nothing
\ padded. The link is the distance BACK from this word's name field to
\ the previous one in the same thread, 1-3 bytes with the tag byte
\ LAST, so it is read backward from the name field:
\     tag < 128           d = tag                                 7 bits
\     128 <= tag < 192    d = (tag AND 63) << 8 OR [nfa-2]        14 bits
\     tag >= 192          d = (tag AND 63) << 16 OR [nfa-2] << 8
\                           OR [nfa-3]                            22 bits
\ and d = 0 ends the thread (CV8-REFERENCE.md 5). Until Iteration 243 the
\ link was a cell and names were padded to one.

: PREV-NFA ( nfa --- nfa' | 0 )
\ The name field of the previous word in this thread, or 0.
  DUP 1- C@                                    ( nfa tag )
  DUP 128 U< IF DUP IF - ELSE 2DROP 0 THEN EXIT THEN
  DUP 192 U< IF 63 AND 8 LSHIFT OVER 2 - C@ OR - EXIT THEN
  63 AND 16 LSHIFT OVER 2 - C@ 8 LSHIFT OR OVER 3 - C@ OR - ;

: NAME=? ( a1 a2 u --- f )
\ Are the u bytes at a1 and a2 equal?
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    >R OVER C@ OVER C@ = 0= IF R> DROP 2DROP 0 EXIT THEN
    1+ SWAP 1+ SWAP R> 1-
  REPEAT DROP 2DROP -1 ;

: SEARCH-WORDLIST ( c-addr u wid --- 0 | xt 1 xt -1)
\ Search the wordlist with address wid for the name c-addr u.
\ Return 0 if not found, the execution token xt and -1 for non-immediate
\ words and xt and 1 for immediate words. Only the thread the name
\ hashes to is walked.
  ROT ROT NAME>BUF
  DUP >R NAMEBUF COUNT R@ @ HASH 1+ CELLS + @ R> DROP   ( nfa | 0 )
  BEGIN DUP WHILE
    \ Bits 5-7 of the count byte are flags, not length.
    DUP C@ 31 AND NAMEBUF C@ = IF
      DUP 1+ NAMEBUF 1+ NAMEBUF C@ NAME=? IF
        DUP NAME> SWAP C@ 64 AND IF 1 ELSE -1 THEN EXIT
      THEN
    THEN
    PREV-NFA
  REPEAT ;

: FIND ( c-addr --- c-addr 0| xt 1|xt -1 )
\ Search all word lists in the search order for the name in the
\ counted string at c-addr. If not found return the name address and 0.
\ If found return the execution token xt and -1 if the word is non-immediate
\ and 1 if the word is immediate.
  #ORDER @ DUP 1 > IF
   CONTEXT #ORDER @ 1- CELLS + DUP @ SWAP CELLBYTES-TOK - @ =
  ELSE 0 THEN
  IF 1- THEN \ If last wordlist is double, don't search it twice.
  BEGIN
   DUP
  WHILE
   1- >R
   DUP COUNT
   R@ CELLS CONTEXT + @ SEARCH-WORDLIST
   DUP
   IF
    R> DROP ROT DROP EXIT \ Exit if found.
   THEN
   DROP R>
  REPEAT
;

\ The following words are related to numeric input.

: DIGIT? ( c -- 0| c--- n -1)
\ Convert character c to its digit value n and return true if c is a
\ digit in the current base. Otherwise return false.
  48 - DUP 0< IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > OVER 17 < AND IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 9 > IF 7 - THEN
  DUP BASE @ < 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  -1
;

: >NUMBER ( ud1 c-addr1 u1 --- ud2 c-addr2 u2 )
\ Convert the string at c-addr with length u1 to binary, multiplying ud1
\ by the number in BASE and adding the digit value to it for each digit.
\ c-addr2 u2 is the remainder of the string starting at the first character
\ that is no digit.
  BEGIN
   DUP
  WHILE
   -1 + >R
   COUNT DIGIT? 0=
   IF
    R> 1+ SWAP -1 + SWAP  EXIT
   THEN
   SWAP >R
   >R
   SWAP BASE @ UM* ROT BASE @ * 0 SWAP D+ \ Multiply ud by base.
   R> 0 D+                                \ Add new digit.
   R> R>
  REPEAT
;

: CONVERT ( ud1 c-addr1 --- ud2 c-addr2)
\ Convert the string starting at c-addr1 + 1 to binary. c-addr2 is the
\ address of the first non-digit. Digits are added into ud1 as in >NUMBER
  -1 + -1 >NUMBER DROP ;

: NUMBER? ( c-addr ---- d f)
\ Convert the counted string at c-addr to a double binary number.
\ f is true if and only if the conversion was successful. DPL contains
\ -1 if there was no point in the number, else the position of the point
\ from the right. Special prefixes: # means decimal, $ means hex.
  -1 DPL !
  BASE @ >R
  COUNT
  OVER C@ 45 = DUP >R IF    -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN \ Get any - sign
  OVER C@ 36 = IF 16 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ $ sign for hex.
  OVER C@ 35 = IF 10 BASE ! -1 + SWAP 1 + SWAP THEN   \ # sign for decimal
  DUP  0 > 0= IF  R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Length 0 or less?
  >R >R 0 0 R> R>
  BEGIN
   >NUMBER
   DUP IF OVER C@ 46 = IF -1 + DUP DPL ! SWAP 1 + SWAP ELSE \ handle point.
         R> DROP R> BASE ! 0 EXIT THEN   \ Error if anything but point
       THEN
  DUP 0= UNTIL 2DROP R> IF DNEGATE THEN
  R> BASE ! -1
;

\ PART 9: THE COMPILER

: (ABORT") ( f --- )
\ Runtime part of ABORT"
           IF R> COUNT TYPE SPACE HERE COUNT TYPE CR WARM
           ELSE R> COUNT + >R THEN ;


: ALLOT ( n --- )
\ Allot n extra bytes of memory, starting at HERE to the dictionary.
  DP +! ;

: , ( x --- )
\ Append cell x to the dictionary at HERE.
  HERE ! 1 CELLS ALLOT ;

: C, ( n --- )
\ Append character c to the dictionary at HERE.
  HERE C! 1 ALLOT ;

: ALIGN ( --- )
\ Add as many bytes to the dictionary as needed to align dictionary pointer.
  BEGIN HERE CELLBYTES-TOK 1- AND WHILE 0 C, REPEAT ;

: LINK-LEN ( d --- n )
\ The bytes a link of distance d needs.
  DUP 128 U< IF DROP 1 EXIT THEN
  16384 U< IF 2 ELSE 3 THEN ;

: LINK, ( d n --- )
\ Append a link of exactly n bytes, tag byte last.
  DUP 1 = IF DROP C, EXIT THEN
  2 = IF DUP 255 AND C, 8 RSHIFT 128 OR C, EXIT THEN
  DUP 255 AND C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 16 RSHIFT 192 OR C, ;

VARIABLE HDR-HEAD ( --- a-addr )
VARIABLE HDR-LEN  ( --- a-addr )

: HEADER ( --- )
\ Create a header for a new definition without a code field. The name
\ is parsed first - WORD leaves it counted at HERE - because the link
\ depends on the thread the name hashes to. The link's length depends
\ on the distance, which depends on where the name lands, which
\ depends on the link's length: bound it from above by sizing for a
\ name at HERE+3, so the real distance always fits. Then the name is
\ moved up and the link written into the gap.
  32 WORD DUP FIND IF ." Redefining: " HERE COUNT TYPE CR THEN DROP DROP
  HERE COUNT CURRENT @ @ HASH 1+ CELLS CURRENT @ + @ HDR-HEAD !
  HDR-HEAD @ IF HERE 3 + HDR-HEAD @ - LINK-LEN ELSE 1 THEN HDR-LEN !
  HERE DUP HDR-LEN @ + HERE C@ 1+ CMOVE>
  HDR-HEAD @ IF HERE HDR-LEN @ + HDR-HEAD @ - ELSE 0 THEN HDR-LEN @ LINK,
  HERE LAST !             \ Set LAST so the definition can be linked by REVEAL
  HERE C@ 1+ HERE C@ 128 + HERE C! ALLOT ;
                          \ Allot the name and set bit 7 in the count byte.

: REVEAL ( --- )
\ Add the last created definition to the CURRENT wordlist, at the head
\ of the thread its name hashes to.
  LAST @ DUP COUNT 31 AND CURRENT @ @ HASH
  1+ CELLS CURRENT @ + ! ;



: OP, ( n --- )
\ Append one opcode byte.
  C, ;

VARIABLE LAST-OP ( --- a-addr )
\ Where the last foldable operation starts, or 0.
VARIABLE LAST-LIT ( --- a-addr )
\ Where the last literal starts, or 0...
VARIABLE LAST-LITV ( --- a-addr )
\ ...and its value.
VARIABLE LAST-CALL ( --- a-addr )
\ Where the last call starts, or 0...
VARIABLE LAST-CALLXT ( --- a-addr )
\ ...and its target.

\ The compiler rewrites four two-operation patterns into one opcode
\ (CV8-REFERENCE.md 7): `LIT n +` and `LIT n =` into ADDI/EQI, and
\ `var @` and `var !` into VAR@/VAR!. It remembers where the last
\ literal and the last call started, and the second half of a pattern
\ rewinds HERE over the first. NOTHING MAY BRANCH BETWEEN THE TWO, so
\ everything that makes HERE a branch target, or emits anything that is
\ not one of the halves, calls NO-PEEP. `;` folds a final operation and
\ its EXIT into one opcode under the same rule.
: NO-PEEP ( --- )
  0 LAST-OP ! 0 LAST-LIT ! 0 LAST-CALL ! ;

: REWIND ( a-addr --- )
\ Discard everything compiled from a-addr to HERE.
  HERE - ALLOT ;

: W, ( n --- )
\ Append a 16-bit little-endian operand.
  DUP 255 AND C, 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, ;

: SLOT, ( a-addr --- )
\ A slot operand naming a-addr, compiled right after its opcode: the
\ offset from the operand itself, two bytes big-endian if it fits 15
\ signed bits, else three with the top bit set - 23 signed bits. It was
\ the offset from START until Iteration 259, and half of the shell's
\ slots needed the long form; from here nine in ten are short.
  HERE -
  DUP -4194304 < OVER 4194303 > OR ABORT" slot beyond reach"
  DUP -16384 < OVER 16383 > OR IF
    DUP 16 RSHIFT 127 AND 128 OR C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C,
  ELSE
    DUP 8 RSHIFT 127 AND C, 255 AND C,
  THEN ;

VARIABLE STATE ( --- a-addr)
\ Variable that holds the compiler state, 0 is interpreting 1 is compiling.

: ]  ( --- )
\ Start compilation mode.
  1 STATE ! ;

: [  ( --- )
\ Leave compilation mode.
  0 STATE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LIT, ( n --- )
\ Append a literal in the narrowest form that holds it.
  NO-PEEP HERE LAST-LIT ! DUP LAST-LITV !
  DUP 0 = IF DROP 97 OP, EXIT THEN              \ push 0
  DUP 1 = IF DROP 98 OP, EXIT THEN              \ push 1
  DUP -1 = IF DROP 99 OP, EXIT THEN             \ push -1
  DUP 256 U< IF HERE LAST-OP ! LIT8-TOK OP, C, EXIT THEN
  DUP 65536 U< IF HERE LAST-OP ! [OP] LIT OP, W, EXIT THEN
  \ A 64-bit cell holds values a signed 32-bit field cannot.
  DUP 2147483648 + 4294967296 U< CELLBYTES-TOK 4 = OR IF
    LIT32-TOK OP, DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, EXIT
  THEN
  125 OP, DUP DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, 32 RSHIFT DUP W, 16 RSHIFT W, ;

: LITERAL ( n --- )
\ Add a literal to the current definition.
  LIT, ; IMMEDIATE

VARIABLE FOLD-OPS ( --- a-addr )
\ The primitives with a folded "then EXIT" opcode, as opcode bytes, in
\ the order of the engine's table.
FOLD-TABLE

: FOLD-OP ( op --- op2 -1 | op 0 )
\ The folded opcode for op, if it has one.
  DUP LIT8-TOK = IF DROP LIT8X-TOK -1 EXIT THEN
  DUP 121 = OVER 123 = OR IF 1+ -1 EXIT THEN   \ ADDI, EQI
  #FOLD-TOK 0 DO
    DUP FOLD-OPS I + C@ = IF DROP I FOLD-BASE-TOK + -1 UNLOOP EXIT THEN
  LOOP 0 ;

: EXIT, ( --- )
\ Append a return, folded into the last operation where that has a
\ folded form. Only ever at the END of a body, so nothing can branch to
\ the EXIT that folding removes. A final 0 or 1 becomes LIT8 so that it
\ folds too.
  LAST-LIT @ IF LAST-LITV @ 2 U< IF
    LAST-LIT @ REWIND HERE LAST-OP ! LIT8-TOK OP, LAST-LITV @ C,
  THEN THEN
  LAST-OP @ ?DUP IF
    DUP C@ FOLD-OP IF SWAP C! ELSE 2DROP [OP] EXIT OP, THEN
  ELSE [OP] EXIT OP, THEN
  NO-PEEP ;

: CALL, ( a-addr --- )
\ A call to the body at a-addr: its offset from the image base, two
\ bytes if it fits 14 bits, else three. The call scale is 0, because
\ bodies are not aligned (CV8-REFERENCE.md 3.4).
  NO-PEEP HERE LAST-CALL ! DUP LAST-CALLXT !
  START @ -
  DUP 16384 < IF DUP 8 RSHIFT 128 OR C, 255 AND C,
  ELSE DUP 16 RSHIFT 192 OR C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C, THEN ;

: PEEP-IMM ( op --- f )
\ Rewrite `LIT n +` or `LIT n =` just compiled, if op completes it.
  LAST-LIT @ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-LITV @ 128 + 256 U< 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP [OP] + = IF DROP 121 ELSE
  [OP] = = IF 123 ELSE 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-LIT @ REWIND LAST-LITV @ >R NO-PEEP
  HERE LAST-OP ! OP, R> 255 AND C, -1 ;

: PEEP-VAR ( op --- f )
\ Rewrite `var @` or `var !` just compiled, where var is a plain data
\ word: its body starts with DOVAR, so its parameter field is fixed.
  LAST-CALL @ 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  LAST-CALLXT @ C@ DOVAR-TOK = 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP [OP] @ = IF DROP 100 ELSE
  [OP] ! = IF 101 ELSE 0 EXIT THEN THEN
  LAST-CALL @ REWIND LAST-CALLXT @ >R NO-PEEP
  OP, R> 4 + ALIGNED SLOT, -1 ;   \ >BODY, which is defined later

: DOES-FETCH? ( xt --- f )
\ Is xt a `CREATE ... DOES> @ ;` word - a buffer from pool.4's BUFFER:,
\ say? Its body is DODOES and a far call to the tail, and DOES> starts
\ every tail with R> (DODOES leaves the parameter field on the return
\ stack), so this tail is R> and the folded @;EXIT.
  DUP C@ DODOES-TOK = 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  1+ DUP C@ 63 AND 16 LSHIFT OVER 1+ C@ 8 LSHIFT OR SWAP 2 + C@ OR
  START @ +
  DUP C@ [OP] R> = 0= IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  1+ C@  [OP] @ FOLD-OP DROP = ;

: COMPILE, ( xt --- )
\ Add the execution semantics of xt to the current definition. A word
\ with the MACRO bit is a primitive or an opcode word: its body starts
\ with its opcode, or with ESC and a selector, and that is inlined.
\ Anything else is a call.
  DUP >NAME C@ 32 AND IF
    DUP C@ 126 = IF
      NO-PEEP DUP C@ OP, 1+ C@ OP,
    ELSE
      C@ DUP PEEP-IMM IF DROP EXIT THEN
      DUP PEEP-VAR IF DROP EXIT THEN
      NO-PEEP HERE LAST-OP ! OP,
    THEN
  ELSE
    \ A word whose DOES> part is `@ ;` compiles to VAR@ on its data
    \ field: one dispatch where the call, the DOES> prologue, the fetch
    \ and the return were four - 6.7% of the shell's loop was that, for
    \ its buffers (Iteration 260).
    DUP DOES-FETCH? IF
      NO-PEEP 100 OP, 4 + ALIGNED SLOT, NO-PEEP EXIT
    THEN
    CALL,
  THEN ;

VARIABLE CSP ( --- a-addr )
\ This variable is used for stack checking between : and ;

VARIABLE 'LEAVE ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable is used for LEAVE address resolution.

: !CSP ( --- )
\ Store current stack pointer in CSP.
   SP@ CSP ! ;

: ?CSP ( --- )
\ Check that stack pointer is equal to value contained in CSP.
   SP@ CSP @ - ABORT" Incomplete control structure" ;

: ; ( --- )
\ Finish the current definition by adding a return to it, make it
\ visible and leave compilation mode.
  EXIT, [ ?CSP REVEAL ; IMMEDIATE

: CREATE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Create a definition that returns its parameter field address when
\ executed. Storage can be added to it with ALLOT. The body is
\ [DOVAR][three bytes][pad to a cell]: DOES> overwrites the front with
\ [DODOES][three-byte call], and the parameter field, which the engine
\ computes as align(body+4), does not move.
  HEADER REVEAL NO-PEEP DOVAR-TOK OP, 0 C, 0 C, 0 C, ALIGN ;

: VARIABLE ( "ccc" --- )
\ Create a variable where 1 cell can be stored. When executed it
\ returns the address.
  CREATE 0 , ;

: CONSTANT ( x "ccc" ---)
\ Create a definition that returns x when executed.
  HEADER REVEAL LIT, EXIT, ;

: OPERAND3@ ( a --- u )   \ a three-byte operand, high byte first
  DUP C@ 16 LSHIFT OVER 1+ C@ 8 LSHIFT OR SWAP 2 + C@ OR ;

: (POSTPONE) ( --- )
\ Followed by the xt to compile, three bytes from START (Iteration 258:
\ it was a cell relative to itself, aligned).
  R> DUP 3 + >R OPERAND3@ START @ +
  DUP >NAME C@ 64 AND IF EXECUTE ELSE COMPILE, THEN
;

: : ( "ccc" --- )
\ Start a new definition, enter compilation mode.
  !CSP HEADER ] ;

: >MARK ( --- a-addr )
\ Emit a placeholder branch operand and leave its address. A branch
\ operand is a signed 16-bit offset from the operand itself.
  NO-PEEP HERE 0 W, ;

: >RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
\ Patch the operand at a-addr so its branch lands on HERE.
  NO-PEEP HERE OVER - SWAP 2DUP C! 1+ SWAP 8 RSHIFT SWAP C! ;

: BACK, ( dest op16 op8 --- )
\ Compile a backward branch to dest. Its distance is known, so it takes
\ the one-byte-offset form when that reaches - nine backward branches
\ in ten (Iteration 258). Both offsets count from the operand.
  NO-PEEP ROT HERE 1+ -                 ( op16 op8 off )
  DUP -128 < IF
    SWAP DROP SWAP OP, W,
  ELSE
    SWAP OP, C, DROP
  THEN NO-PEEP ;

: BEGIN ( --- x )
\ Start a BEGIN UNTIL or BEGIN WHILE REPEAT loop.
  NO-PEEP HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: UNTIL ( x --- )
\ Form a loop with matching BEGIN.
\ Runtime: A flag is take from the stack
\ each time UNTIL is encountered and the loop iterates until it is nonzero.
  [OP] ?BRANCH 0BRANCH8-TOK BACK, ; IMMEDIATE

: AGAIN ( x --- )
\ End a BEGIN AGAIN loop: branch back unconditionally.
  [OP] BRANCH BRANCH8-TOK BACK, ; IMMEDIATE

: IF    ( --- x)
\ Start an IF THEN or IF ELSE THEN construction.
\ Runtime: At IF a flag is taken from
\ the stack and if it is true the part between IF and ELSE is executed,
\ otherwise the part between ELSE and THEN. If there is no ELSE, the part
\ between IF and THEN is executed only if flag is true.
  NO-PEEP [OP] ?BRANCH OP, >MARK ; IMMEDIATE

: THEN ( x ---)
\ End an IF THEN or IF ELSE THEN construction.
  >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: ELSE ( x1 --- x2)
\ part of IF ELSE THEN construction.
  NO-PEEP [OP] BRANCH OP, >MARK SWAP >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: WHILE  ( x1 --- x2 x1 )
\ part of BEGIN WHILE REPEAT construction.
\ Runtime: At WHILE a flag is taken from the stack. If it is false,
\  the program jumps out of the loop, otherwise the part between WHILE
\  and REPEAT is executed and the loop iterates to BEGIN.
  POSTPONE IF SWAP ; IMMEDIATE

: REPEAT ( x1 x2 --- )
\ part of BEGIN WHILE REPEAT construction.
  [OP] BRANCH BRANCH8-TOK BACK, >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

VARIABLE POCKET ( --- a-addr )
\ Buffer for S" strings that are interpreted.
  252 ALLOT-T

: '  ( "ccc" --- xt)
\ Find the word with name ccc and return its execution token.
  32 WORD FIND 0= ABORT" Not found" ;

: ['] ( "ccc" ---)
\ Compile the execution token of the word with name ccc as a literal.
  ' LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE

: CHAR ( "ccc" --- c)
\ Return the first character of "ccc".
  BL WORD 1 + C@ ;

: [CHAR] ( "ccc" --- )
\ Compile the first character of "ccc" as a literal.
  CHAR LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE

: DO ( --- x)
\ Start a DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: ( n1 n2 --- ) start a loop with initial count n2 and
\ limit n1.
  POSTPONE (DO) NO-PEEP 'LEAVE @ HERE 0 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE-LINK, ( --- )
\ The operand of a branch to the loop's exit, not known yet. Until the
\ loop ends, the operands of its ?DO and LEAVEs form a chain from 'LEAVE,
\ each holding the distance back to the one before (0 ends it).
  'LEAVE @ ?DUP IF HERE SWAP - ELSE 0 THEN
  HERE 'LEAVE ! W, ;

: ?DO ( --- x )
\ Start a ?DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: ( n1 n2 --- ) start a loop with initial count n2 and
\ limit n1. Exit immediately if n1 = n2.
  POSTPONE (?DO) [OP] BRANCH OP, 'LEAVE @ 0 'LEAVE ! LEAVE-LINK,
  NO-PEEP HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE ( --- )
\ Runtime: leave the matching DO LOOP immediately - drop the loop and
\ branch to its exit.
  POSTPONE UNLOOP [OP] BRANCH OP, LEAVE-LINK, NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

: RESOLVE-LEAVE ( --- )
\ Point every branch in the chain at HERE, the loop's exit.
  'LEAVE @
  BEGIN ?DUP WHILE
    DUP W@ ?DUP IF OVER SWAP - ELSE 0 THEN     ( a next )
    SWAP >RESOLVE
  REPEAT ;

: LOOP  ( x --- )
\ End a DO LOOP.
\ Runtime: Add 1 to the count and if it is equal to the limit leave the loop.
  POSTPONE (LOOP) [OP] BRANCH BRANCH8-TOK BACK,
  RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

: +LOOP ( x --- )
\ End a DO +LOOP
\ Runtime: ( n ---) Add n to the count and exit if this crosses the
\ boundary between limit-1 and limit.
  POSTPONE (+LOOP) [OP] BRANCH BRANCH8-TOK BACK,
  RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

: RECURSE ( --- )
\ Compile a call to the current (not yet finished) definition.
  LAST @ NAME> CALL, ; IMMEDIATE

: STRING, ( c-addr u --- )
\ A counted string in the code, unpadded: the code is bytes, and the
\ runtimes return to the byte after it (Iteration 258).
  DUP C, HERE OVER ALLOT SWAP MOVE ;

: ."  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: type that string.
   POSTPONE (.") 34 PARSE STRING, ; IMMEDIATE


: S"  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: ( --- c-addr u) Return start address and length of that string.
  STATE @ IF POSTPONE (S") 34 PARSE STRING,
             ELSE 34 PARSE POCKET PLACE POCKET COUNT THEN ; IMMEDIATE

: ABORT"  ( "ccc<quote>" --- )
\ Parse a string delimited by " and compile the following runtime semantics.
\ Runtime: ( f --- ) if f is nonzero, print the string and abort program.
  POSTPONE (ABORT") 34 PARSE STRING, ; IMMEDIATE

: ABORT ( --- )
\ Abort unconditionally without a message.
1 ABORT" " ;

: POSTPONE ( "ccc" --- )
  POSTPONE (POSTPONE) ' START @ -
  DUP 16 RSHIFT 255 AND C, DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, 255 AND C,
  NO-PEEP ; IMMEDIATE

: IMMEDIATE ( --- )
\ Make last definition immediate, so that it will be executed even in
\ compilation mode.
  LAST @ DUP C@ 64 OR SWAP C! ;

: ( ( "ccc<rparen>" --- )
\ Comment till next ).
  BEGIN
    41 DUP PARSE + C@ = 0=
\    [CHAR] ) DUP PARSE + C@ = 0=
  WHILE
    REFILL 0= IF EXIT THEN
  REPEAT
; IMMEDIATE

: \
\ Comment till end of line.
  SOURCE >IN ! DROP ; IMMEDIATE

: >BODY ( xt --- a-addr)
\ Convert execution token to parameter field address: past DOVAR or
\ DODOES and three bytes, aligned. See CREATE.
  4 + ALIGNED ;

: (;CODE) ( --- )
\ Runtime for DOES>: make the last created word call the code after
\ the call to this word, and end the defining word (R> discards its
\ return address). The body becomes [DODOES][three-byte call to the
\ tail], in the room CREATE left. Always the far form: at a call scale
\ of 0 the near form reaches only 16 KB.
  R> LAST @ NAME>                     ( tail xt )
  DODOES-TOK OVER C! 1+ SWAP          ( a-addr tail )
  START @ -                           ( a-addr v )
  2DUP 255 AND SWAP 2 + C!
  2DUP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP 1+ C!
  16 RSHIFT 192 OR SWAP C! ;

: DOES>  ( --- )
\ Word that contains DOES> will change the behavior of the last created
\ word such that it pushes its parameter field address onto the stack
\ and then executes whatever comes after DOES>. DODOES leaves the
\ parameter field on the return stack for the tail's R>.
  POSTPONE (;CODE) NO-PEEP POSTPONE R> ; IMMEDIATE

\ PART 10: TOP LEVEL OF INTERPRETER

: ?STACK ( ---)
\ Check for stack over/underflow and abort with an error if needed.
  DEPTH DUP 0< SWAP 10000 > OR ABORT" Stack error" ;

: INTERPRET ( ---)
\ Interpret words from the current source until the input source is exhausted.
  BEGIN
   32 WORD DUP C@
  WHILE
   FIND DUP
   IF
    -1 = STATE @ AND
    IF
     COMPILE,
    ELSE
     EXECUTE
    THEN
   ELSE DROP
    NUMBER? 0= ABORT" Undefined word" DROP
    STATE @ IF LITERAL THEN
   THEN  ?STACK
  REPEAT   DROP
;

: EVALUATE ( c-addr u --- )
\ Evaluate the string c-addr u as if it were typed on the terminal.
  SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R  >IN @ >R
  #SRC ! SRC ! 0 >IN ! -1 SID ! INTERPRET
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! ;

VARIABLE INCLUDE-BUFFER ( --- a-addr)
\ This is the buffer where the lines of included files are stored.
508 ALLOT-T

VARIABLE INCLUDE-POINTER ( --- a-addr)
\ This variable holds the address where the included line is stored.

: INCLUDE-FILE ( fid --- )
\ Read lines from the file identified by fid and interpret them.
\ INCLUDE and EVALUATE nest in arbitrary order.
  INCLUDE-POINTER @ >R SID @ >R SRC @ >R #SRC @ >R >IN @ >R
  #SRC @ INCLUDE-POINTER +! INCLUDE-POINTER @ SRC !
  SID !
  BEGIN
   REFILL
  WHILE
   INTERPRET
  REPEAT
  R> >IN ! R> #SRC ! R> SRC ! R> SID ! R> INCLUDE-POINTER !
;

: INCLUDED  ( c-addr u ---- )
\ Open the file with name c-addr u and interpret all lines contained in it.
  R/O OPEN-FILE ABORT" Can't open include file"
  DUP >R INCLUDE-FILE
  R> CLOSE-FILE DROP
;

: QUIT ( --- )
\ This word resets the return stack, resets the compiler state, the include
\ buffer and then it reads and interprets terminal input.
  R0 @ RP! [
  TIB SRC ! 0 SID !
  INCLUDE-BUFFER INCLUDE-POINTER !
  BEGIN
   REFILL DROP INTERPRET STATE @ 0= IF ." OK" THEN CR
  0 UNTIL
;

: WARM ( ---)
\ This word is called when an error occurs. Clears the stacks, sets
\ BASE to decimal, closes the files and resets the search order.
  R0 @ RP! S0 @ SP! DECIMAL
  2 #ORDER !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CONTEXT CELL+ !
  FORTH-WORDLIST CURRENT !
  QUIT ;

: RELOCATE-WORDLIST ( wid --- )
\ Add START to every thread head of one wordlist.
\
\ Cell 0 is the thread COUNT and must not be touched; an empty thread
\ is 0 and must STAY 0, or it becomes a pointer to the image base.
\ SAVE-SYSTEM's SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST is this in reverse and the two
\ have to agree.
  DUP @ 1+ 1 DO
    DUP I CELLS + DUP @ ?DUP IF START @ + SWAP ! ELSE DROP THEN
  LOOP DROP ;

: COLD ( --- )
\ The first word that is called at the start of Forth.
  START ! START @ DP +!
  FORTH-WORDLIST RELOCATE-WORDLIST
  \ ...and every other wordlist, down the chain WORDLIST built.
  WORDLISTS @
  BEGIN ?DUP WHILE
    START @ + DUP RELOCATE-WORDLIST
    1 CELLS - @
  REPEAT
  SP@ S0 !
  RP@ CELL+ R0 ! \ Initialize variables SO and RO
  BOOT @ ?DUP IF START @ + EXECUTE THEN
  \ A turnkey image's own boot word normally never returns (shell.4's
  \ MAIN either exits or runs its own read-eval loop). If one does
  \ return, fall through to the ordinary interactive behavior rather
  \ than doing something surprising.
  ." Welcome to Forth " CR
  WARM ;

END-CROSS
